    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Excavations show settlement on the site from the 10th century BCE, while the Mahāvaṃsa places the founding of the city in 437 BCE and tells that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandukabhaya">Pandukabhaya</a> made it the capital of the Sinhalese kingdom.</li><li>Anuradhapura was the Sinhalese capital for about 1,500 years and a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a>, with a network of temples and monasteries that now covers over 100 square kilometres.</li><li>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> invasions of 993 and 1014 the city was largely deserted and the capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polonnaruwa">Polonnaruwa</a>, but it stayed a pilgrimage site and was revived under British rule from the 1870s.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Jaya Sri Maha Bodhi, a tree at Anuradhapura, is held to be the oldest still-living, documented, planted tree in the world and is believed to have originated from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodhi-tree">Bodhi tree</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodh-gaya">Bodh Gaya</a>, under which the Buddha attained enlightenment.</p></aside>
    </section>

